of the library that  we'd  kind of designed  and redesigned  and redesigned the library's  requirements just like kept changing and  different  people working at different parts of the  library had their inputs and i presented  the design once again and they  said something like  also by the way we just want to let you  know that we really appreciate you being  patient with us and uh we know  uh we've gone through this process back  and forth  and them just saying that that made me  feel really good i am a sucker for those  compliments  if you find your client annoying because  the client is slashing your budget and i  think like anything you just  have to put yourself in their shoes if  you had entrusted someone with millions  of dollars like wouldn't you make these  requests if you think it's reasonable  then just like try to take the emotion  out of it try to look at it objectively  my question  is  what are the points we should focus on  while explaining to jury so that they  could understand our perspective  more easier brackets in hierarchy  so i would start with do the like the  why how and what so why present the  problem like site conditions client  requirements and then how how you're  solving the problem  and then the what the what is your  building  and  if you're kind of looking for a  hierarchical list in terms of like the  most convincing  how  i would say  function is probably your secret weapon  as an architect  function is  how you move around where people gather  your circulation path your views all the  pragmatic things about a project that  clients hire you  to solve  and then there's the material  and i think unless there's a very strong  functional reason for why your material  is the main solution to solve your  problem  this should be  yeah second in hierarchy in terms of  most convincing  especially if you're doing a school  project  i always found like  the material as a solution as not very  convincing because you know when you're  in school you're not really working with  manufacturers or actual  like reps of the materials right i think  this should supplement your functional  argument but it should not be  the only reason and then third is form  i've seen architects who design things  by eye  and they make design decisions based on  their aesthetic preference  but  i find this a lot harder to sell to the  client and also  it's a harder solve for your employees  or colleagues who have to work on the  project form should follow function  and not the other way around the fourth  in the hierarchy i would say is probably  like the mood or the feeling  and yeah i would say this is probably  the least convincing argument in terms  of like providing an actual solution to  your  your conditions  um for your jury  but you know also keep in mind this is  how a lot of designers work like they  saw the mood and the feeling  and you know like especially with  interior design like clients eat that up  so  it really depends on like what kind of  audience you're talking to  could you give me some tips on  undergraduate students trying to apply  to schools like ryerson ubc and waterloo  and some tips some portfolio tips  on what the committee really looks for  so if you haven't watched my portfolio  video check that out you are very lucky  my friend  because i actually sat on the portfolio  review committee when i was in my  master's  and i spent many days reviewing uh  undergrad portfolios the portfolios that  have had the best feedback are the ones  that had really good sketches in drawing  when i say good sketches like sketches  where you can see the thought process  and where you can see the person's  interpretation of an object or a  building or a space if i could give you  one tip right now i would say  try to build your sketching skills  looks like i have a fangirl  my question is is it true that you'll  have to sacrifice a lot of things like  quality time with your friends and  family self-care and reading more books  and etc  yes  but also  not necessarily when i was in  architecture school i  definitely made a lot of sacrifices i  didn't  make a lot of time for my  non-architecture friends and family  looking back i was um  let's just say i'm really grateful that  i  have amazing and gracious friends every  single one of them were super  understanding and like nobody really  gave me [ __ ] for it that was just like a  phase of my life that i  had to go through for me you know i made  friends in architecture school and i was  just like really plunged into that world  but i can say that when i started  working i definitely had more time for  other things in my life compared to  being in architecture school and you  know i i always try to make time for  self-care  uh before i go to bed i  have my nightly routine and i try to do  things that i really enjoy doing i make  sure to  finish working  whether it's like my architecture work  or  or youtube or emails  i  try to make sure to finish the night at  7 30  but yeah i do wish i had more time for  reading  for me  making this youtube channel actually was  a huge time sucker and i i realized i  had to  uh reorganize my priorities a lot i was  amazed at how much time  i saved by not watching tv every night  what would you suggest for someone who's  taken a four year long hiatus from  architecture  edit  i mean specifically advice for getting  back into the field what to say during  interviews during the gap  what one can do with their outdated  portfolio  with the portfolio part um i'll just  refer you to my  how to create an architecture portfolio  video try to do competitions or try to  do your own projects to fill that up uh  yeah so actually i've asked a follow-up  question to jessica  who  actually took a break from architecture  due to mental health reasons actually  jessica i don't think you are the only  one i think this question is very  important when i was at my old firm we  had this girl  come in for an interview she had all the  right kinds of skills  but she had a three  year long break when my boss asked her  she kind of said oh i just needed some  time off and that was live  when she left we all thought  she had the right kind of qualifications  that kind of raised a lot of questions  my boss said something like she seemed  like she was burnt out and she didn't  get the job this is kind of tricky  and i think there's a lot of different  ways to approach this and i think it  really depends on how you frame your  break and how you present yourself i'm  not saying this to make you nervous yeah  i think the way you formulate your  answer  can either  really work to your advantage  or it can give off  a bad impression if you  really want to go back to work in  architecture  you have to frame it in a way that gives  you more value  in that sense it's like  how you would reframe  any other work experience you can say  things like  i decided to take some time off because  i was burnt out of my old job and i  think you can be honest about that  and  in fact i think if the employer doesn't  want to hire you because they think  burning out means you can't handle the  workload then i don't think you want to  work there  yeah so maybe you can say  like oh i took some time off because i  was like completely burnt out  but  taking these fears off  and doing other things gave me  time and space to pursue other interests  that shows that you're  multifaceted and you're flexible it gave  me time to reflect on my relationship  with work  and i worked on building healthy habits  so it shows that you're  reflective and resilient gave me time to  reconnect with my passion for  architecture  or find new interesting areas in  architecture  show that you  you're passionate you're freshly  passionate what i found is that  architects really value life experience  like i know an architect who left  architecture  and worked in film for like five years  and he came back and he's now working as  a senior architect even though he only  has a couple of years of experience in  like actual architecture just like try  to spin it in a way that gives you more  experience if you were doing something  for those four years  maybe it's related to architecture and  maybe it's not anyway like i would try  to find a way to connect what you were  doing  during that time to your role you said  you're a fellow canadian  and i don't know like where exactly you  are but uh like at least here in bc the  job market for young architects is like  very very good i'm sure you're going to  be able to find a place that's going to  be able to  give you good  work-life balance the if the first place  that you go to wasn't a great fit  like you can leave it's a hot market for  architects  and  um there's just like  yeah there's tons of places that are  just begging to fill seats yeah good  luck any hints on how to be a good  interior designer so i'm not an interior  designer but i have worked as an  architect at an interior design firm  with other interior designers i think  interior designers have so many skills  that we don't have they're like  amazing in their own right i've actually  been wanting to make a video on this  like the difference between architecture  and  interior design and how that  influences the design process they're  very different from the way they come up  with ideas  to  um like how we present the ideas to be a  good interior designer you need to be  really good at reading the client you  need to be good at turning something  that's very elusive  like feelings and moods  and like branding elements and like  colors  and  turn them into like a material palette  and color palette  and spatial arrangements  because they don't have the hard  constraints like architecture  they don't have things like zoning or  building codes or like site conditions  to play with so they're really just like  selling a mood and a feeling and then  yeah that would be the other  important skill selling yourself and  selling your work because if you're  convincing the client of a mood or a  feeling  you need to be very expressive with your  words  and not be shy with your words i have  actually decided to split this video  into several parts i wanted to dig into  these questions and answers a little bit  deeper  and in fact i did ask you guys whether  you would prefer  a live or if you would prefer me to  like go into these questions deeper and  88 of you guys answered um like a full  deep dive video it's actually pretty  late now so i need to get some sleep i'm  gonna continue this tomorrow i'll see  you in the next video  you
 

